      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "jupyter: stats\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Python Basics for `sklearn`\n",
        "\n",
        "This note introduces only the Python syntax needed for the rest of these notes. The goal is not to cover Python as a full programming language. The goal is to make code using `numpy`, `matplotlib`, and `sklearn` readable.\n",
        "\n",
        "The intended reader already knows R. Many ideas are the same: objects have names, functions take arguments, vectors and matrices are central, and modeling workflows usually follow a fit-then-predict pattern.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Running Python Code\n",
        "\n",
        "A Python code chunk looks like this:"
      ],
      "id": "5557ab3e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x = 10\n",
        "x"
      ],
      "id": "c05a4ec2",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Assignment uses `=`."
      ],
      "id": "3f98a471"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "a = 2\n",
        "b = 3\n",
        "a + b"
      ],
      "id": "7dfa96be",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Python comments start with `#`."
      ],
      "id": "04ccae5d"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# This is a comment.\n",
        "x = 5"
      ],
      "id": "0895ed9c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Unlike R, Python uses zero-based indexing. The first element has index `0`."
      ],
      "id": "fa5ae6b9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "values = [10, 20, 30]\n",
        "values[0]"
      ],
      "id": "eccabd88",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Importing Packages\n",
        "\n",
        "Python packages are loaded with `import`.\n",
        "\n",
        "The usual imports in these notes are:"
      ],
      "id": "330d8263"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt"
      ],
      "id": "8f1165a0",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This means:\n",
        "\n",
        "- `numpy` is used as `np`;\n",
        "- `pandas` is used as `pd`;\n",
        "- `matplotlib.pyplot` is used as `plt`.\n",
        "\n",
        "Specific functions or classes can be imported from a package:"
      ],
      "id": "0274cd24"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n",
        "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split"
      ],
      "id": "b8a86499",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This is similar in spirit to loading a function from an R package, but Python usually names the module path explicitly.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Basic Objects\n",
        "\n",
        "Python has several basic data types."
      ],
      "id": "72e5bcef"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x = 3          # integer\n",
        "y = 2.5        # float\n",
        "name = \"tree\"  # string\n",
        "flag = True    # Boolean"
      ],
      "id": "1a1a1243",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Use `type()` to check the type of an object."
      ],
      "id": "7e1347e0"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "type(y)"
      ],
      "id": "8f4ec9b7",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Python is case-sensitive. `x` and `X` are different objects.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Lists\n",
        "\n",
        "A list is an ordered collection of objects."
      ],
      "id": "6ff5d035"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "features = [\"age\", \"income\", \"education\"]\n",
        "features"
      ],
      "id": "dd82c42d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Lists can contain objects of different types, although in data analysis we usually keep them simple."
      ],
      "id": "f9aa13c3"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "mixed = [1, \"a\", True]\n",
        "mixed"
      ],
      "id": "31b2594e",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Indexing starts at 0."
      ],
      "id": "af389c69"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "features[0]"
      ],
      "id": "a98da802",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Use negative indexing to count from the end."
      ],
      "id": "e808ac84"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "features[-1]"
      ],
      "id": "8e1a862f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Slicing uses `start:stop`, where `stop` is not included."
      ],
      "id": "156502fe"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "features[0:2]"
      ],
      "id": "08882c60",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This returns the first two elements.\n",
        "\n",
        "Some common list operations are:"
      ],
      "id": "4ff26f5c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "features.append(\"gender\")\n",
        "len(features)"
      ],
      "id": "30597a08",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Lists are often used to specify columns or hyperparameter grids."
      ],
      "id": "a04b79e1"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "num_features = [\"age\", \"chol\", \"thalach\"]\n",
        "k_values = [1, 3, 5, 10]"
      ],
      "id": "bcb3947e",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Dictionaries\n",
        "\n",
        "A dictionary stores key-value pairs. It is similar to a named list in R."
      ],
      "id": "7ebe1dd9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "params = {\n",
        "    \"n_neighbors\": 5,\n",
        "    \"weights\": \"uniform\",\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "params"
      ],
      "id": "22bbe41f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Access a value by its key:"
      ],
      "id": "7c6fca52"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "params[\"n_neighbors\"]"
      ],
      "id": "586d85f8",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Dictionaries are used frequently in `sklearn`, especially for parameter grids."
      ],
      "id": "64fe9cd5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "param_grid = {\n",
        "    \"max_depth\": [2, 3, 4],\n",
        "    \"min_samples_leaf\": [1, 5, 10],\n",
        "}"
      ],
      "id": "727fe849",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "For pipelines, parameter names often use two underscores:"
      ],
      "id": "d9bc888a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "param_grid = {\n",
        "    \"model__C\": [0.1, 1, 10],\n",
        "}"
      ],
      "id": "b3859dc3",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This means the parameter `C` inside the pipeline step named `\"model\"`.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Functions\n",
        "\n",
        "Python functions are called with parentheses."
      ],
      "id": "30d0fff1"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "round(3.14159, 2)"
      ],
      "id": "422aac22",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Named arguments use `name=value`."
      ],
      "id": "ae394b7b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "np.linspace(0, 1, num=5)"
      ],
      "id": "7b4ac5d1",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Many `sklearn` objects are created by calling a class with arguments:"
      ],
      "id": "1805d1c3"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "model = LinearRegression()\n",
        "model"
      ],
      "id": "bfa5cac4",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Indentation\n",
        "\n",
        "Python uses indentation to mark code blocks. R uses braces `{}`; Python uses indentation.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, a `for` loop:"
      ],
      "id": "75254c58"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "for k in [1, 3, 5]:\n",
        "    print(k)"
      ],
      "id": "cd921d72",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The indented line belongs to the loop. These notes use loops only when they are helpful for plotting or comparing models.\n",
        "\n",
        "## `numpy.array`\n",
        "\n",
        "The most important data structure for numerical computation is `numpy.array`."
      ],
      "id": "a1f8e61e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "\n",
        "x = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4])\n",
        "x"
      ],
      "id": "2d00a464",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "An array has a shape."
      ],
      "id": "2ba2c9e5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x.shape"
      ],
      "id": "b944771c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "For this object, the shape is `(4,)`. This means it is a one-dimensional array with 4 elements.\n",
        "\n",
        "### One-Dimensional Arrays\n",
        "\n",
        "A one-dimensional array behaves like a vector."
      ],
      "id": "84a44ee9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x = np.array([10, 20, 30])\n",
        "x.ndim"
      ],
      "id": "0d43c836",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x.shape"
      ],
      "id": "86442896",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Indexing is zero-based."
      ],
      "id": "b424458a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x[0]"
      ],
      "id": "6dee7104",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Vectorized arithmetic works similarly to R."
      ],
      "id": "254e91bc"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x + 1"
      ],
      "id": "edb049a3",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x * 2"
      ],
      "id": "64618c61",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "np.mean(x)"
      ],
      "id": "a56c98aa",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Two-Dimensional Arrays\n",
        "\n",
        "A two-dimensional array is like a matrix."
      ],
      "id": "9152a41b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X = np.array(\n",
        "    [\n",
        "        [1, 2],\n",
        "        [3, 4],\n",
        "        [5, 6],\n",
        "    ]\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "X"
      ],
      "id": "8301422d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Check the dimension and shape."
      ],
      "id": "34c68921"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X.ndim"
      ],
      "id": "0215e97f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X.shape"
      ],
      "id": "da442268",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The shape is `(3, 2)`, meaning 3 rows and 2 columns.\n",
        "\n",
        "Indexing uses `[row, column]`."
      ],
      "id": "7d9856fa"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X[0, 1]"
      ],
      "id": "ab058baa",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This is the entry in row 0 and column 1.\n",
        "\n",
        "Select a column:"
      ],
      "id": "2aad4505"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X[:, 0]"
      ],
      "id": "35d93354",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Select a row:"
      ],
      "id": "c3e212e9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X[1, :]"
      ],
      "id": "88772fb7",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The colon `:` means \"all indices\" in that direction.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Dimensions and `sklearn`\n",
        "\n",
        "The most common Python issue in `sklearn` is array dimension.\n",
        "\n",
        "In `sklearn`, the predictor matrix `X` should be two-dimensional:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X:\\quad n\\times p.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The response vector `y` should usually be one-dimensional:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "y:\\quad n.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This means:"
      ],
      "id": "4df887ff"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X = np.array(\n",
        "    [\n",
        "        [1.0],\n",
        "        [2.0],\n",
        "        [3.0],\n",
        "        [4.0],\n",
        "    ]\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "y = np.array([1.2, 1.9, 3.1, 3.8])\n",
        "\n",
        "X.shape, y.shape"
      ],
      "id": "b934390a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Here `X` has shape `(4, 1)`, not `(4,)`.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Why `(n, 1)` Matters\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose we have one predictor."
      ],
      "id": "84231ed7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x = np.array([1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0])\n",
        "x.shape"
      ],
      "id": "91f5e275",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This has shape `(4,)`, so `sklearn` treats it as a one-dimensional object, not as a predictor matrix.\n",
        "\n",
        "Use `.reshape(-1, 1)` to convert it to a matrix with one column."
      ],
      "id": "05db4075"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X = x.reshape(-1, 1)\n",
        "X.shape"
      ],
      "id": "226b0d0a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The `-1` means \"infer this dimension from the data.\" In this case, Python infers 4 rows.\n",
        "\n",
        "This pattern appears often in the notes:"
      ],
      "id": "7f301e38"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "test_x = np.linspace(0, 1, 101).reshape(-1, 1)\n",
        "test_x.shape"
      ],
      "id": "a18443eb",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Flattening Back to One Dimension\n",
        "\n",
        "Sometimes we want a one-dimensional array for plotting."
      ],
      "id": "78b07def"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X[:, 0]"
      ],
      "id": "eb22cbf4",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "or"
      ],
      "id": "35a8d3e7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X.reshape(-1)"
      ],
      "id": "d5b47776",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Both produce a one-dimensional array.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Arrays versus Lists\n",
        "\n",
        "Lists are general containers. Arrays are numerical objects."
      ],
      "id": "11bc0f30"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "[1, 2, 3] + [4, 5, 6]"
      ],
      "id": "c2cd6919",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "List addition concatenates."
      ],
      "id": "7e25a533"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "np.array([1, 2, 3]) + np.array([4, 5, 6])"
      ],
      "id": "1069e803",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Array addition is elementwise.\n",
        "\n",
        "For modeling and numerical work, use arrays or pandas data frames, not plain lists.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Random Numbers\n",
        "\n",
        "These notes often use `numpy` random number generators for simulation."
      ],
      "id": "4c3a1c21"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "rng = np.random.default_rng(1)\n",
        "\n",
        "x = rng.normal(size=5)\n",
        "x"
      ],
      "id": "0c8bb2e6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The number `1` is a seed. It makes the random output reproducible.\n",
        "\n",
        "Some common random functions are:"
      ],
      "id": "ee83007b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "rng.normal(loc=0, scale=1, size=5)"
      ],
      "id": "379bdf25",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "rng.uniform(0, 1, size=5)"
      ],
      "id": "d633dd4e",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "For a two-dimensional predictor matrix:"
      ],
      "id": "ab13abd1"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X = rng.normal(size=(10, 3))\n",
        "X.shape"
      ],
      "id": "121f0614",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Basic `sklearn` Workflow\n",
        "\n",
        "Most `sklearn` models use the same workflow.\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Create the model object.\n",
        "2. Fit the model with `.fit(X_train, y_train)`.\n",
        "3. Predict with `.predict(X_test)`.\n",
        "4. For classifiers, get probabilities with `.predict_proba(X_test)`.\n",
        "\n",
        "Example:"
      ],
      "id": "f10b9169"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n",
        "from sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\n",
        "from sklearn.datasets import load_iris\n",
        "\n",
        "iris = load_iris()\n",
        "\n",
        "X = iris.data\n",
        "y = iris.target\n",
        "\n",
        "X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\n",
        "    X,\n",
        "    y,\n",
        "    test_size=0.3,\n",
        "    random_state=1,\n",
        "    stratify=y,\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "model = KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=5)\n",
        "model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
        "\n",
        "pred = model.predict(X_test)\n",
        "prob = model.predict_proba(X_test)\n",
        "\n",
        "pred[:5]"
      ],
      "id": "2e8368af",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Here:\n",
        "\n",
        "- `X_train` and `X_test` are two-dimensional arrays;\n",
        "- `y_train` and `y_test` are one-dimensional arrays;\n",
        "- `pred` contains predicted class labels;\n",
        "- `prob` contains predicted class probabilities.\n",
        "\n",
        "Check shapes:"
      ],
      "id": "ffe656b9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X_train.shape, y_train.shape, pred.shape, prob.shape"
      ],
      "id": "651507f2",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Train-Test Split\n",
        "\n",
        "`train_test_split` is the standard way to create training and test sets."
      ],
      "id": "f31f9649"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n",
        "\n",
        "X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\n",
        "    X,\n",
        "    y,\n",
        "    test_size=0.3,\n",
        "    random_state=1,\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "ec154fb9",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "For classification, use `stratify=y` to preserve class proportions."
      ],
      "id": "86f8d481"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\n",
        "    X,\n",
        "    y,\n",
        "    test_size=0.3,\n",
        "    random_state=1,\n",
        "    stratify=y,\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "5cc63cc3",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This is similar to making a random split in R, but the function returns all four objects at once.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Pipelines\n",
        "\n",
        "A pipeline chains preprocessing and modeling steps."
      ],
      "id": "51545767"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\n",
        "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n",
        "from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n",
        "\n",
        "pipe = Pipeline(\n",
        "    steps=[\n",
        "        (\"scaler\", StandardScaler()),\n",
        "        (\"model\", LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000)),\n",
        "    ]\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "pipe.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
        "pipe.predict(X_test)"
      ],
      "id": "b0a46231",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Each step is a pair:"
      ],
      "id": "38d47367"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "(\"step_name\", object)"
      ],
      "id": "7e738a92",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Pipeline parameters use two underscores."
      ],
      "id": "1b5be43a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "pipe.get_params()[\"model__max_iter\"]"
      ],
      "id": "c9f3a5f1",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Cross-Validation and Grid Search\n",
        "\n",
        "Cross-validation evaluates a model over several train-validation splits."
      ],
      "id": "bd56f2a3"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\n",
        "\n",
        "scores = cross_val_score(\n",
        "    pipe,\n",
        "    X,\n",
        "    y,\n",
        "    cv=5,\n",
        "    scoring=\"accuracy\",\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "scores"
      ],
      "id": "490dc3f6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The mean score is:"
      ],
      "id": "d4371a49"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "scores.mean()"
      ],
      "id": "c962b44a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Grid search tries several hyperparameter values."
      ],
      "id": "49dd972e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.model_selection import GridSearchCV\n",
        "\n",
        "param_grid = {\n",
        "    \"model__C\": [0.1, 1, 10],\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "grid = GridSearchCV(\n",
        "    pipe,\n",
        "    param_grid=param_grid,\n",
        "    cv=5,\n",
        "    scoring=\"accuracy\",\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "grid.fit(X, y)\n",
        "grid.best_params_"
      ],
      "id": "1d421ec1",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The best fitted model is:"
      ],
      "id": "fc24226f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "grid.best_estimator_"
      ],
      "id": "382b7f2f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## `matplotlib` Basics\n",
        "\n",
        "These notes use only basic `matplotlib`.\n",
        "\n",
        "The standard import is:"
      ],
      "id": "c9c052a3"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt"
      ],
      "id": "1c10bff2",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Scatter Plot"
      ],
      "id": "13e654fa"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4])\n",
        "y = np.array([1.1, 2.0, 2.8, 4.2])\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.scatter(x, y)\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"x\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"y\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Scatter Plot\")\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "6edf63f4",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Line Plot"
      ],
      "id": "c61e4df9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x_grid = np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, 200)\n",
        "y_grid = np.sin(x_grid)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.plot(x_grid, y_grid)\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"x\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"sin(x)\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Line Plot\")\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "cb5c45ad",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Scatter and Line Together"
      ],
      "id": "50039367"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "rng = np.random.default_rng(1)\n",
        "\n",
        "x = rng.uniform(0, 2 * np.pi, size=30)\n",
        "y = np.sin(x) + rng.normal(scale=0.2, size=30)\n",
        "x_grid = np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, 200)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.scatter(x, y, facecolors=\"none\", edgecolors=\"black\")\n",
        "plt.plot(x_grid, np.sin(x_grid), color=\"C1\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"x\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"y\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Data and Curve\")\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "c394f555",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This pattern appears often when we plot data points and a fitted curve.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Multiple Plots\n",
        "\n",
        "Use `plt.subplots` for multiple panels."
      ],
      "id": "0c1b3d29"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "fig, axs = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(10, 4))\n",
        "\n",
        "axs[0].scatter(x, y)\n",
        "axs[0].set_title(\"scatter\")\n",
        "\n",
        "axs[1].plot(x_grid, np.sin(x_grid))\n",
        "axs[1].set_title(\"line\")\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "0f3de1db",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Notice that when using axes objects, we write `ax.set_title()` instead of `plt.title()`.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Histograms"
      ],
      "id": "21ba689f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "z = rng.normal(size=200)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.hist(z, bins=20, edgecolor=\"black\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"z\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"count\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Histogram\")\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "b7150695",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Common Errors\n",
        "\n",
        "### One-Dimensional `X`\n",
        "\n",
        "The most common error is passing a one-dimensional predictor array to `sklearn`.\n",
        "\n",
        "Bad:"
      ],
      "id": "54c38c9c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "x = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4])\n",
        "model.fit(x, y)"
      ],
      "id": "dc821c47",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Good:"
      ],
      "id": "7bc6d7cb"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "X = x.reshape(-1, 1)\n",
        "model.fit(X, y)"
      ],
      "id": "aa329ab8",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Forgetting Parentheses\n",
        "\n",
        "In Python, methods need parentheses."
      ],
      "id": "8cbb95e5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
        "model.predict(X_test)"
      ],
      "id": "3cedad0e",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Writing `model.predict` without parentheses refers to the method itself, but does not call it.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Class Name versus Model Object\n",
        "\n",
        "Another common mistake is confusing an `sklearn` class with an object created from that class.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, `DecisionTreeClassifier` is the class itself."
      ],
      "id": "da7bc64e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\n",
        "\n",
        "DecisionTreeClassifier"
      ],
      "id": "39a897d6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "To create a model object, call the class with parentheses."
      ],
      "id": "8f025e88"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "model = DecisionTreeClassifier()"
      ],
      "id": "888f13f9",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Arguments go inside the parentheses."
      ],
      "id": "cc3abb91"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "model = DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=3)\n",
        "model.fit(X_train, y_train)"
      ],
      "id": "d4998da6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Do not write:"
      ],
      "id": "c6249721"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "model = DecisionTreeClassifier\n",
        "model.fit(X_train, y_train)"
      ],
      "id": "c73f1456",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The first version creates an estimator object. The second version only stores the class itself, so `.fit()` will not work as intended.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Mixing Lists and Arrays\n",
        "\n",
        "Use arrays for elementwise numerical operations."
      ],
      "id": "63532eee"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "np.array([1, 2, 3]) * 2"
      ],
      "id": "5f79bd88",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Plain lists do not always behave like numerical vectors.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Summary\n",
        "\n",
        "For the rest of these notes, the most important Python ideas are:\n",
        "\n",
        "- indexing starts at 0;\n",
        "- lists store ordered objects;\n",
        "- dictionaries store key-value pairs;\n",
        "- `numpy.array` is the basic numerical vector or matrix object;\n",
        "- `X` in `sklearn` should be two-dimensional;\n",
        "- `y` is usually one-dimensional;\n",
        "- `.reshape(-1, 1)` converts one predictor into a one-column matrix;\n",
        "- `sklearn` models use `.fit()`, `.predict()`, and often `.predict_proba()`;\n",
        "- `matplotlib.pyplot` is used for basic scatter plots, line plots, histograms, and subplots."
      ],
      "id": "797ec1f5"
    }
